    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Honda Has a Taste of the e-MTB World with the RN0 Motor-Assisted Bicycle\nHonda Confirms V6 Hybrid Production Investment Into Alabama Plant\n18 photos\nPhoto: Honda\nThe Honda Alabama complex has spent a quarter of a century building pretty much everything from minivans to pickup trucks, but the Japanese automaker wants to give it more room to work. As part of a $462 million commitment, American Honda Motor is making a few changes to its Lincoln facility, of which a V6 hybrid powertrain and a new assembly line are the most relevant.\nIn the process, that investment will also result in the addition of 200 jobs. Those $462 million will also see production line 1 reworked to handle Passport assembly. The Pilot's cooler sibling is currently made on line 2 exclusively. While that sounds like a fairly simple adjustment, bear in mind that Honda builds a lot of things at its Alabama plant.\nAAP currently handles Odyssey, Pilot, Passport, and Ridgeline assembly. On the engine side of things, both the SOHC J35Y6 and the DOHC J35Y8 naturally aspirated 3.5-liter V6 mills are born there. Speaking of engine production, the Alabama Auto Plant handles aluminum die casting, precision CNC machining, and engine assembly per se.\nFor a company that is dealing with changing demand across four nameplates, the ability to build the Passport on two lines is definitely worth having. This ability gives AAP production planners more options when they need to adjust Passport output. American Honda Motor hasn't disclosed how much additional capacity this change will unlock.\nRegarding the assembly line for the V6 hybrid powertrain, that one is all the more important when you remember how popular hybrids are nowadays in the United States market. Also worth noting, American Honda Motor is very serious about hybrids after scaling back its electric vehicle targets due to cooling demand.\nPhoto: Honda Per a recent briefing, the Japanese automaker wants to launch 15 new Honda and Acura hybrid models globally by March 2030. The still-mysterious hybrid V6 powertrain is understood to include a newly developed internal combustion engine, similarly new drive units, a next-gen battery pack engineered for heavy loads, and electrified all-wheel drive in the form of an electrified rear axle.\nS+ Shift simulated stepped gear shifts and rev-matching downshifts are coming to Hondas and Acura models as well. Designated Honda Hybrid, this new powertrain promises 10 percent better acceleration and 30 percent better fuel economy than currently sold gasoline-only models. Out back, the inverter-less AWD unit promises to provide some assistance under wide-open throttle acceleration.\nAmerican Honda Motor is further upgrading the Anna Engine Plant in Ohio for the next-generation Atkinson four-pot engine. Similarly, the Honda Transmission Plant in Ohio is prepping to increase hybrid system production capacity by a tremendous 250 percent. Its Georgia counterpart will receive a new transmission assembly line for hybrid systems intended for larger vehicles like the Passport.\nTurning our attention back to the Lincoln facility in Alabama, said complex has produced more than 7 million vehicles and nearly 6.6 million V6 engines since the first Odyssey minivan rolled off the assembly line in November 2001. The site has accumulated $3.2 billion in capital investments so far. Its annual production capacity is 340,000 vehicles and 340,000 engines.\nSince the Alabama plant opened, nearly 15 percent of all Honda and Acura vehicles manufactured in North America come from there. Not surprising in the least, all four vehicles produced there as of November 2026 rank high in the American-Made Index. The highest-ranked model from Honda for 2026 is the Ridgeline in fifth place, followed by the Odyssey in sixth.\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\nHonda V6 Hybrid production US\nRead Press Release\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT 2026 LA Auto Show: 2027 Chrysler Pacifica Goes Copperline and Also Packs a New Ski Grey Interior",
